{"w":2400,"lines":[{"c":1,"h":0.020348836167448092,"x":0.86185566932460023,"t":"Reading","y":0.037790698162525005,"w":0.088659795125325513},{"c":1,"h":0.015998613533382833,"x":0.053608244287873391,"w":0.32783505757649745,"t":"READING PASSAGE 3","y":0.087199060707845555},{"x":0.053608235775296012,"w":0.88041239420572925,"t":"You should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 27-40, which are based on Reading","y":0.12645348889041796,"h":0.020348836167448092,"c":1},{"y":0.14521538903107845,"h":0.018826561399718611,"c":1,"x":0.053305435147322035,"t":"Passage 3 below.","w":0.17790082295735676},{"t":"Neuroaesthetics","w":0.24948453267415366,"h":0.020348836167448092,"c":1,"x":0.37319587812366856,"y":0.18313953536551564},{"t":"An emerging discipline called neuroaesthetics is seeking to bring scientific objectivity to","h":0.017615971814445075,"x":0.051546384320805598,"y":0.22665883723893243,"w":0.83298970540364581,"c":1},{"t":"the study of art, and has already given us a better understanding of many masterpieces.","h":0.017441861012089666,"y":0.24418604623474782,"x":0.04948453543817654,"w":0.83092783610026055,"c":1},{"w":0.86391754150390632,"h":0.017458076835448644,"y":0.26306517924871309,"t":"The blurred imagery of Impressionist paintings seems to stimulate the brain's amygdala, for","c":1,"x":0.049484528165572687},{"c":1,"h":0.019083969465648831,"y":0.28038755143023586,"w":0.89278350830078124,"x":0.049484536287753636,"t":"instance. Since the amygdala plays a crucial role in our feelings, that finding might explain why"},{"t":"many people find these pieces so moving.","x":0.04948453600779288,"w":0.39999999999999997,"y":0.30087209318626251,"h":0.015988371754420583,"c":1},{"w":0.79587631225585942,"h":0.017458930270362383,"t":"Could the same approach also shed light on abstract twentieth-century pieces, from","c":1,"x":0.049484524177426575,"y":0.33720930204972488},{"t":"Mondrian's geometrical blocks of colour, to Pollock's seemingly haphazard arrangements","h":0.015988371754420472,"c":1,"y":0.35610465132971991,"x":0.049484533599584588,"w":0.83917526245117191},{"h":0.018431819752252387,"x":0.049439169170909923,"w":0.83927317301432292,"y":0.37350946140565355,"c":1,"t":"of splashed paint on canvas? Sceptics believe that people claim to like such works simply"},{"c":1,"t":"because they are famous. We certainly do have an inclination to follow the crowd. When","w":0.82886596679687496,"y":0.39389534900445544,"h":0.015988371754420694,"x":0.049484542268750334},{"y":0.41133294185063618,"t":"asked to make simple perceptual decisions such as matching a shape to its rotated image, for","x":0.047422661895786387,"w":0.88247426350911473,"h":0.017446128186658694,"c":1},{"c":1,"t":"example, people often choose a definitively wrong answer if they see others doing the same.","x":0.049484531276054705,"y":0.43012331203425891,"h":0.017615971814445186,"w":0.87835052490234378},{"y":0.44912790714411455,"c":1,"x":0.047422668770186839,"t":"It is easy to imagine that this mentality would have even more impact on a fuzzy concept like","w":0.88041239420572914,"h":0.015988371754420694},{"x":0.049484535159793286,"y":0.46799765124974835,"w":0.54639175415039065,"t":"art appreciation, where there is no right or wrong answer.","h":0.016147974163241319,"c":1},{"w":0.82474227905273434,"h":0.015988371754420583,"c":1,"y":0.50436046528354628,"t":"Angelina Hawley-Dolan, of Boston College, Massachusetts, responded to this debate by","x":0.04948453047232116},{"w":0.88464904785156251,"x":0.047366971131339329,"y":0.52119635132514941,"h":0.018998698773834977,"t":"asking volunteers to view pairs of paintings - either the creations of famous abstract artists or","c":1},{"y":0.5406976741429157,"w":0.86597941080729168,"h":0.017441861012089777,"c":1,"x":0.047422665630926605,"t":"the doodles of infants, chimps and elephants. They then had to judge which they preferred."},{"c":1,"t":"A third of the paintings were given no captions, while many were labelled incorrectly -","x":0.047422688436254724,"w":0.82061854044596338,"h":0.015988371754420583,"y":0.55959302342295469},{"x":0.047422684856150095,"t":"volunteers might think they were viewing a chimp's messy brushstrokes when they were","w":0.83505152384440096,"y":0.57692307709574908,"h":0.017615971814445075,"c":1},{"w":0.87628865559895819,"c":1,"t":"actually seeing an acclaimed masterpiece. In each set of trials, volunteers generally preferred","x":0.045360826849392973,"y":0.59593023228033548,"h":0.017441861012089777},{"w":0.88659790039062503,"h":0.017615971814445075,"x":0.045360843505718361,"y":0.61479741608742178,"c":1,"t":"the work of renowned artists, even when they believed it was by an animal or a child. It seems"},{"c":1,"t":"that the viewer can sense the artist's vision in paintings, even if they can't explain why.","w":0.81649485270182298,"y":0.63358778627014933,"h":0.017615971814445186,"x":0.045360820973530926},{"h":0.017615971814445075,"y":0.66999412828054383,"c":1,"x":0.045360819655019785,"w":0.79381444295247394,"t":"Robert Pepperell, an artist based at Cardiff University, creates ambiguous works that"},{"c":1,"x":0.04742267481115571,"y":0.68895348809621693,"t":"are neither entirely abstract nor clearly representational. In one study, Pepperell and his","w":0.82474227905273445,"h":0.017446981621572544},{"c":1,"w":0.85360819498697926,"x":0.045360851001355798,"y":0.70493247233583289,"t":"collaborators asked volunteers to decide how'powerfulithey considered an artwork to be,","h":0.018904736933817334},{"w":0.85360829671223948,"h":0.017615971814445075,"c":1,"x":0.045360799978329189,"y":0.72519083991321909,"t":"and whether they saw anything familiar in the piece. The longerthey took to answerthese"},{"x":0.047422686082548654,"w":0.85979380289713547,"h":0.015988371754420583,"t":"questions, the more highly they rated the piece under scrutiny, and the greatertheir neural","y":0.74418604667849197,"c":1},{"x":0.045360835923335022,"w":0.83505152384440107,"c":1,"y":0.76159718136341437,"t":"activity. It would seem that the brain sees these images as puzzles, and the harder it is to","h":0.017615971814445075},{"w":0.69690719604492191,"c":1,"t":"decipher the meaning, the more rewarding is the moment of recognition.","x":0.04536083485069009,"h":0.017615971814445075,"y":0.78038755154577211},{"w":0.29149803161621091,"t":"leltsresources.org","h":0.022583953086866559,"x":0.35219443363911673,"c":1,"y":0.95433624864730393},{"h":0.015988371754420583,"c":1,"w":0.032989689509073883,"x":0.90721649534855564,"y":0.94622093034475807,"t":"49"}],"h":3406}